      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm looking for some wine suggestions. What would you recommend for someone who wants to try new things?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some wine suggestions?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Considering you're a wine enthusiast and your grandfather used to have a winery in Great Britain, I'd recommend trying some new wines from regions you might not have explored before. Have you considered wines from the Loire Valley in France or the Douro Valley in Portugal? They offer a",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that's more helpful! Can you recommend something that's gentle on the stomach? I sometimes deal with mild acid reflux, so low-acidity wines would be better for me.",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some wine suggestions?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "You may want to consider wines with lower acidity levels, such as those from the regions I mentioned earlier, like the Loire Valley or the Douro Valley. These wines tend to have a smoother, more rounded taste that might be easier on your stomach. Additionally, you may want to look into wines made from grape varieties like Pinot Noir or Merlot, which are generally known for their lower acidity levels. Just remember to always drink responsibly and in moderation, especially if you're dealing with acid reflux.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That sounds good! I\u2019ve heard of Sauvignon Blanc tasting grassy. Can you give me some wine suggestions that taste like freshly cut grass?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some wine suggestions that tastes like freshly cut grass? ",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "I think you may be confusing the taste of Sauvignon Blanc with the smell of freshly cut grass. Sauvignon Blanc is known for its citrus and grassy notes, but the grassy taste is more of a herbaceous or vegetal flavor. If you're looking for wines that taste like freshly cut grass, you might want to consider wines from the Loire Valley in France, such as Sancerre or Pouilly-Fum\u00e9. These wines are made from the Sauvignon Blanc grape and are known for their crisp, grassy flavors. However, keep in mind that the grassy taste can be a bit overpowering for some people, so it's always a good idea to try a small amount first to see if you enjoy it.  (",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great explanation! Between Sauvignon Blanc, Terruzi & Puthod Terre di Tufi, and Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote, which would you say is more gentle for someone with acid reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Which of these wines, the Sauvignon Blanc, the Terruzi & Puthod Terre di Tufi, and the Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote, are best for someone with stomach acid reflux? ",
          "user_rubrik_score": 4,
          "text": "I would recommend the Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote. Although Sancerre wines are known for their acidity, the Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote is a more balanced wine with a smoother taste. The wine is made from the Sauvignon Blanc grape and has a rich, complex flavor profile with notes of citrus, stone fruit, and a hint of grassiness. The acidity is still present, but it's not as sharp as some other Sancerre wines. Additionally, the wine is made from grapes grown in the La Grande Cote vineyard, which is known for its limestone soils that can help to reduce the acidity of the wine. Overall, I think this wine would be a good choice for someone with acid reflux who wants to try a Sancerre wine.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Nice! Switching topics a bit\u2014where is Oaked Chardonnay wine made in Canada?",
          "user_rubrik": "Where is the Oaked Chardonnay wine made in Canada?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "You're looking for Oaked Chardonnay wine made in Canada. Canada has a growing wine industry, and many wineries produce high-quality Chardonnay wines. Some notable regions for Chardonnay production in Canada include the Okanagan Valley in British Columbia and the Niagara Peninsula in Ontario. In the Okanagan Valley, wineries like Mission Hill Family Estate and Quails' Gate Winery produce excellent Oaked Chardonnay wines. In the Niagara Peninsula, wineries like Inniskillin Wines and Peller Estates Winery also produce great Oaked Chardonnay wines. These wines often have a rich, buttery flavor profile with notes of vanilla, caramel, and oak, which are characteristic of Oaked Chardonnay. I recommend trying some of these wines to see which one you enjoy the most!  (",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
