    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for some good wine suggestions. Can you help me pick something?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some wine suggestions?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "absolutely. since you \u2019 re avoiding sugar, aiming to lose weight, and have occasional reflux, focus on dry, lower - alcohol wines ( ideally \u226412 % abv ). great picks to try : - cool \u2011 climate pinot noir ( ontario, okanagan, willamette, burgundy ) : light, low tannin, versatile ; about 3. 4 g carbs per 5 oz. aim for \u226412 \u2013 12. 5 % abv. - dry italian rose ( montepulciano / sangiovese ) : bright berry / herbal, food \u2011 friendly ; look for 11 \u2013 12 % abv. - gamay / beaujolais ( beaujolais - villages or cru like fleurie ) : juicy, low tannin, easy on the stomach ; often 12 \u2013 12. 5 % abv. - if reflux is calm and you want bubbles : brut nature / extra brut cava or cremant ( very dry, typically \u226412 % abv ). skip bubbles if they trigger reflux. shopping tips : - choose \u201c dry \u201d and check abv ; lower alcohol generally means less sugar. - look up the winery tech sheet and aim for residual sugar under ~ 2",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0011-45-12118:3": -3.195406198501587,
            "clueweb22-en0046-45-10278:2": -3.2860641479492188,
            "clueweb22-en0029-39-17015:3": -3.5335354804992676,
            "clueweb22-en0033-12-05620:5": -3.5500550270080566,
            "clueweb22-en0004-45-07385:4": -4.041804790496826
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, those sound great! I\u2019m curious\u2014are there any wines that taste kind of like freshly cut grass?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some wine suggestions that tastes like freshly cut grass? ",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "absolutely \u2014 \u201c fresh - cut grass \u201d is classic in certain dry whites : - sauvignon blanc - marlborough, new zealand : vivid grass, gooseberry, lime ( aim for 11 \u2013 12. 5 % abv ). - loire valley, france ( sancerre, pouilly - fume, touraine ) : lean, mineral, nettle / herb notes. - canada : niagara or okanagan sauvignon blanc often shows crisp, herbaceous character. - verdejo ( rueda, spain ) : grassy, fennel, citrus ; look for \u201c seco \u201d and ~ 11. 5 \u2013 12. 5 % abv. - hunter valley semillon ( australia ) : lemon - lime and lemongrass ; often 10 \u2013 11 % abv and very dry. - gruner veltliner ( austria ) : green herbs / pea shoots, white pepper ; typically dry around 12 % abv. - vinho verde ( portugal ; loureiro / arinto ) : citrusy, green - herbal at 9 \u2013 11 % abv ; slight spritz \u2014 skip if bubbles aggravate reflux. for low sugar and weight goals : choose \u201c dry \u201d and target 10 \u2013 12 %",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0036-12-04611:6": -4.384190559387207,
            "clueweb22-en0022-30-16868:5": -4.860530853271484,
            "clueweb22-en0033-12-05620:5": -4.863442420959473,
            "clueweb22-en0033-12-05620:6": -4.887417793273926,
            "clueweb22-en0036-12-04611:5": -4.8897247314453125
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Super helpful, thanks! Of Sauvignon Blanc, Terruzi & Puthod Terre di Tufi, and Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote\u2014do any stand out as better for someone with acid reflux issues?",
          "user_rubrik": "Which of these wines, the Sauvignon Blanc, the Terruzi & Puthod Terre di Tufi, and the Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote, are best for someone with stomach acid reflux? ",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short answer : pick a dry sauvignon blanc at 11 \u2013 12 % abv. it \u2019 s easiest to keep alcohol ( a common reflux trigger ) and calories low while staying zero / very low sugar. - domaine pascal cotat sancerre la grande cote : beautiful but typically high \u2011 acid and often 13 % + abv \u2014 more likely to aggravate reflux. - teruzzi & puthod terre di tufi : rounder, oak \u2011 softened acidity can feel gentler, but abv is usually 13 \u2013 13. 5 %, which may trigger reflux if alcohol is your main issue. - best fit : a low \u2011 abv sauvignon blanc ( aim 11 \u2013 11. 5 % ). examples : crumsa terres rares sauvignon blanc ( 11. 5 % abv, 0 g sugar, very low calories ) or largesse sauvignon blanc ( 11. 5 % abv, 0 g sugar ). tips to minimize reflux : - have a small glass with food, not on an empty stomach. - serve well chilled to soften perceived acidity. - if acidity still bites, consider similarly dry but softer",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0010-78-16295:0": -2.359293222427368,
            "clueweb22-en0038-48-12650:0": -2.7378478050231934,
            "clueweb22-en0007-88-11375:2": -2.8282809257507324,
            "clueweb22-en0010-78-16295:1": -3.0514845848083496,
            "clueweb22-en0038-48-12650:1": -3.255082130432129
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Got it, that makes sense. Now I\u2019m interested in Oaked Chardonnay\u2014where is it made in Canada?",
          "user_rubrik": "Where is the Oaked Chardonnay wine made in Canada?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "You\u2019ll find standout oaked Chardonnay in two main Canadian regions:  - Ontario (Niagara Peninsula): Many producers barrel\u2011ferment/age Chardonnay here. Look for Featherstone Estate Winery (Niagara) \u2014 they even use Canadian oak barrels, giving a style between the richness of American oak and the leaner French oak profile.  - British Columbia (Okanagan Valley): While many Okanagan Chardonnays are delicately oaked, there are celebrated richer, oak\u2011influenced bottlings, especially from warmer southern sites.   - Notable producers/cuv\u00e9es: Checkmate Artisanal Winery (Attack, Queen\u2019s Advantage, Knight\u2019s Challenge, Little Pawn, Capture, Fool\u2019s Mate) from areas like Golden Mile Bench and Black Sage/Osoyoos; Quail\u2019s Gate Stewart Family Reserve Chardonnay (West Kelowna/Skaha area).  Quick tip for your reflux/fitness goals: oaked Chardonnays often sit around 13% ABV; choose the lower\u2011ABV options when possible and enjoy with food, well\u2011chilled.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0041-56-06591:1": 0.009193092584609985,
            "clueweb22-en0007-48-10803:5": -1.1430656909942627,
            "clueweb22-en0022-04-12330:4": -1.2051869630813599,
            "clueweb22-en0037-43-03744:0": -1.3101319074630737,
            "clueweb22-en0041-82-15026:1": -1.567097544670105
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "The Okanagan Valley comes up a lot\u2014what is the Okanagan Valley in British Columbia and is this a good place to spend some free time?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the Okanagan Valley in British Columbia and is this a good place to spend some free time?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "the okanagan valley is british columbia \u2019 s major wine and lake district, centered around okanagan lake ( kelowna, penticton, vernon down to oliver / osoyoos ). it \u2019 s one of canada \u2019 s top wine regions, known for chardonnay, merlot, syrah and riesling, with standout producers like mission hill, quail \u2019 s gate, checkmate, painted rock, burrowing owl, le vieux pin, summerhill, and cedarcreek. is it a good place to spend free time? absolutely. you can : - taste at scenic wineries and vineyard restaurants or join guided wine tours around west kelowna, naramata bench, oliver & osoyoos, and lake country. - enjoy lakeside parks and beaches ( e. g., waterfront park, city park ), and easy trails like mission creek regional park. - bike the historic kettle valley railway at myra canyon or take relaxed sightseeing cruises and tours. practical tips for your goals : choose dry whites around 11 \u2013 12 % abv ( many okanagan sauvignon blancs and chardonnays fit ), sip with food, and skip bubbly if ref",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0006-96-02889:0": -1.300934076309204,
