    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Very helpful! Could you tell me where oaked Chardonnay is made in Canada?",
          "user_rubrik": "Where is the Oaked Chardonnay wine made in Canada?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Canada makes oaked Chardonnay primarily in these regions:  - British Columbia: Okanagan Valley (Naramata, Golden Mile, Black Sage Bench), Similkameen Valley and Vancouver Island/Cowichan \u2014 warmer sites and many producers barrel\u2011ferment or age in oak for richer styles.   - Ontario: Niagara Peninsula (Niagara-on-the-Lake, Beamsville Bench, Twenty Mile Bench), Lake Erie North Shore and Prince Edward County \u2014 Niagara and Lake Erie sites often yield fuller, oaked Chardonnays; PEC is cooler but some producers use oak for a richer profile.   - Small amounts elsewhere: New Brunswick and Nova Scotia have producers, but oak use is less common.  When shopping look for labels saying \u201cbarrel\u2011fermented,\u201d \u201coak\u2011aged,\u201d \u201cmalolactic fermentation,\u201d \u201cbuttery/vanilla\u201d or \u201csur lie + batonnage\u201d (lees stirring adds richness). Ask your retailer for \u201coaked\u201d or \u201cbuttery\u201d Canadian Chardonnays from Okanagan or Niagara.",
