    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m looking for some wine suggestions! Can you help with that?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some wine suggestions?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Absolutely \u2014 here are practical, reflux- and weight-conscious picks that should still let you enjoy a nightly glass for sleep:  - Whites / sparkling (lower calories, often gentler): dry Pinot Grigio, Sauvignon Blanc, or a dry Brut sparkling wine.   - Reds (pairs with dark chocolate; pick dry, lower-alcohol options): Merlot or Pinot Noir.   - Look for lower-alcohol, \u201cnatural\u201d or low-sugar labels if possible \u2014 they tend to be gentler on reflux and have fewer calories per glass.    Tips for reflux and weight goals: - Stick to one standard glass (\u22485 oz), sip slowly and earlier in the evening (avoid drinking right before bed).   - Avoid sweet wines and late heavy meals.   - If you notice symptoms, try chilled whites or lower\u2011alcohol bottles and consider keeping a brief food/drink diary to spot triggers.  If you want, tell me whether you prefer red or white and I\u2019ll suggest a few specific bottles available in Canada.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0039-08-15095:4": -0.6109230518341064,
            "clueweb22-en0001-89-04963:6": -0.6460291147232056,
            "clueweb22-en0026-86-09256:8": -0.9617339372634888,
            "clueweb22-en0007-65-10299:9": -0.9813642501831055,
            "clueweb22-en0025-33-16952:10": -1.03989839553833
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! That\u2019s very helpful. I\u2019m also interested in wines that taste like freshly cut grass\u2014can you recommend something with that flavor?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you give me some wine suggestions that tastes like freshly cut grass? ",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great choice \u2014 \u201cfreshly cut grass\u201d is a classic herbaceous note. Try these styles:  - Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc (New Zealand) \u2014 the archetype for grassy, zingy aromas.   - Sancerre or Pouilly\u2011Fum\u00e9 (Loire, France) \u2014 cooler\u2011climate Sauvignon Blancs with crisp, flinty green-herb character.   - Verdejo (Rueda, Spain) \u2014 grassy and citrusy, often a bit rounder than NZ.   - Gr\u00fcner Veltliner (Austria) \u2014 green-pepper, herbaceous, and sometimes grassy.  Look for unoaked, cool\u2011climate and \u201cherbaceous\u201d or \u201cgreen\u201d on tasting notes; choose lower\u2011alcohol bottlings when possible. For your reflux and weight goals: serve about 5 oz chilled, sip earlier in the evening, and start with a small pour to see how you feel. If you want, tell me whether you prefer New World (NZ) or Old World (France/Spain/Austria) and I\u2019ll suggest specific bottles available in Canada.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0015-97-13437:4": -4.09298038482666,
            "clueweb22-en0015-97-13437:3": -4.753476142883301,
            "clueweb22-en0015-97-13437:1": -4.831755638122559,
            "clueweb22-en0015-97-13437:2": -5.514257907867432,
            "clueweb22-en0006-72-02275:12": -6.313117980957031
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s fantastic\u2014thanks! Between Sauvignon Blanc, Terruzi & Puthod Terre di Tufi, and Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote, which would be the easiest on mild reflux?",
          "user_rubrik": "Which of these wines, the Sauvignon Blanc, the Terruzi & Puthod Terre di Tufi, and the Domaine Pascal Cotat Sancerre La Grande Cote, are best for someone with stomach acid reflux? ",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Terre di Tufi is likely the gentlest on mild reflux. Warmer-climate Italian whites (and many Italian styles labeled Terre/terre di) tend to show riper fruit and a rounder mid\u2011palate with less razor\u2011sharp acidity than classic Sauvignon/Sancerre.    Sancerre (Domaine Pascal Cotat) and bright Sauvignon Blancs\u2014especially Marlborough styles\u2014are typically higher in acidity and herbaceousness, which can more easily provoke reflux.    Tips to reduce risk: choose the lowest ABV you can find (around or under ~12.5%), pick an unoaked or lightly oaked bottling, serve a modest 5\u2011oz pour chilled (not ice\u2011cold), sip earlier in the evening, and avoid drinking right before bed. Try a small taste first and keep a short diary to confirm what works best for you.",
          "citations": {
